{"ex-205-1":{"number":"1","title":"Moment in pulling a gate","question":"<p>A boy pulls a gate with a force of 10 N in different ways as shown in the following top-view diagrams. Find the moment of the force about the hinge in each case.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/a3f3214b9e9efccfdacd7444.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:472.3px\"/></p>","answer":"<p>Apply τ = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>F</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>d</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> sin θ.</p><p>(a) Moment = 10 × 0.8 × sin 90° = 8 N m (clockwise)</p><p>(b) Moment = 10 × 0.8 × sin 60° = 6.93 N m (anticlockwise)</p><p>(c) Moment = 10 × 0.8 × sin 120° = 6.93 N m (clockwise)</p><p>(d) Moment = 10 × 0.8 × sin 180° = 0</p>","tips":"<p><b>Tip</b></p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/2e645ecd56160ea90e2211b4.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:185.5px\"/></p><p><b>Tip</b></p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/495c3d67f5d42027da455ade.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:185.5px\"/></p>","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 2  |  Chapter 205  |  Source PDF p. 5","source":{"name":"205 Examples.docx","sha256":"215c6a15e4c92f2dd297cc2ee7e95a57a309760a3a81d9ef8f88b21a4fef01d1","block":2},"id":"ex-205-1","chapter":"205"},"ex-205-2":{"number":"2","title":"Net moment on a wheel","question":"<p>Children <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>Q</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> turn a wheel of radius 0.2 m. Find the net moment acting on the wheel about its centre <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>O</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> in each of the following cases.</p><p>(a) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> pushes with 3 N upwards and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>Q</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> pushes with 5 N downwards (Fig a)</p><p>(b) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> pushes with 3 N upwards and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>Q</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> pushes with 5 N upwards (Fig b)</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/945a4ebfaaff9b8ed75d94cf.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:526.1px\"/></p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/71b55c6597b0be3406636f0c.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:41.4px\"/></p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/e4d322225b03cec983da091c.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:42.2px\"/></p>","answer":"<p>Take the clockwise direction as positive. Apply τ = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>F</mtext></mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>d</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>⊥</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>.</p><p>(a) τ<sub>net</sub> = 3 × 0.2 + 5 × 0.2 = 1.6 N m</p><p>The net moment is 1.6 N m (clockwise).</p><p>(b) τ<sub>net</sub> = 3 × 0.2 − 5 × 0.2 = −0.4 N m</p><p>The net moment is 0.4 N m (anticlockwise)</p>","tips":"","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 2  |  Chapter 205  |  Source PDF p. 7","source":{"name":"205 Examples.docx","sha256":"215c6a15e4c92f2dd297cc2ee7e95a57a309760a3a81d9ef8f88b21a4fef01d1","block":17},"id":"ex-205-2","chapter":"205"},"ex-205-3":{"number":"3","title":"Which ruler is in equilibrium?","question":"<p>A light ruler <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>B</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> which is 50 cm long is initially at rest on a smooth horizontal table. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>O</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> is the mid-point of the ruler. The following top-view diagrams show two forces acting on the ruler in three different ways.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/c9354058c8c7b84514b8f3ab.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:536.9px\"/></p><p>(a) Find the net force and the net moment acting on the ruler about <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>O</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> in each case. Take the direction towards the right and the clockwise direction as positive.</p><p>(b) The ruler in one of the above cases is in equilibrium. Which case is it?</p>","answer":"<div class=\"study-table-wrap\"><table><tbody><tr><td></td><td><p>(i)</p></td><td><p>(ii)</p></td><td><p>(iii)</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Net force</p></td><td><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>F</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>e</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = 5 − 5 = 0</p></td><td><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>F</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>e</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = 5 + 5 = 10 N</p></td><td><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>F</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>e</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = 5 − 5 = 0</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Net moment about <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>O</mtext></mrow></mrow></math></p></td><td><p>τ<sub>net</sub> = 5 × 0.25 − 5 × 0.25 = 0</p></td><td><p>τ<sub>net</sub> = 5 × 0.25 − 5 × 0.25 = 0</p></td><td><p>τ<sub>net</sub> = −5 × 0.25 −5 × 0.25 = −2.5 N m</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Net moment about <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></math></p></td><td><p>τ<sub>net</sub> = 0</p></td><td><p>τ<sub>net</sub> = 5 × 0.5 = 2.5 N m</p></td><td><p>τ<sub>net</sub> = −5 × 0.5 = −2.5 N m</p></td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>(a)</p><p>(b) Case (i)</p>","tips":"<p><b>Tip</b></p><p>There is no net force and no net moment about different points on the ruler in this case.</p>","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 2  |  Chapter 205  |  Source PDF p. 12","source":{"name":"205 Examples.docx","sha256":"215c6a15e4c92f2dd297cc2ee7e95a57a309760a3a81d9ef8f88b21a4fef01d1","block":31},"id":"ex-205-3","chapter":"205"},"ex-205-4":{"number":"4","title":"Tree trunk in equilibrium","question":"<p>A monkey walks on a fallen tree trunk which is supported horizontally by two stones at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>Y</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>. The stones are 1 m apart. The monkey’s weight is 300 N. The trunk’s weight is 240 N and its centre of gravity is 0.4 m from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>. The monkey is 0.1 m to the left of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>. The trunk remains at rest.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/e533c19c5f2c7de07356e843.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:484.6px\"/></p><p>(a) The stones at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>Y</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> exert vertical forces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>F</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>F</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>Y</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> on the tree trunk. Find the magnitude of these two forces.</p><p>(b) The monkey walks to the left to pick some bananas. What is its maximum distance <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>d</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> without making the trunk topple?</p>","answer":"<p>(a) Free-body diagram for the tree trunk:</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/e3137edf709b1ece612080a7.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:250.7px\"/></p><p>Take moment about <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>Y</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>.</p><p>Total clockwise moments = total anticlockwise moments</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>F</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> × 1 = 300 × (0.1 + 1) + 240 × (1 − 0.4)</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>F</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = 474 N</p><p>Take moment about <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>.</p><p>Total clockwise moments = total anticlockwise moments</p><p>240 × 0.4 = 300 × 0.1 + <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>F</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>Y</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> × 1</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>F</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>Y</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = 66 N</p><p>(b) Free-body diagram for the tree trunk:</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/34d1d623d02b547aa3a4235a.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:250.7px\"/></p><p>Take moment about <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>.</p><p>Total clockwise moments = total anticlockwise moments</p><p>240 × 0.4 = 300 × <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>d</mtext></mrow></mrow></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>d</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 0.32 m</p>","tips":"<p><b>Skill</b></p><p>Problem-solving</p><p>Object in equilibrium</p><p class=\"numbered-step\">① Draw a free-body diagram for the object.</p><p class=\"numbered-step\">② Set up the equations using <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>F</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>e</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = 0.</p><p class=\"numbered-step\">③ Set up equations using τ<sub>net</sub> = 0 about any point. Choose a point to set an equation with one unknown if possible.</p><p class=\"numbered-step\">④ Solve the equation(s).</p><p><b>Tip</b></p><p>As the moment provided by F<sub>Y</sub> is 0, this equation has one unknown only.</p><p><b>Tip</b></p><p>If the monkey walks too close to the left end, the tree trunk will turn anticlockwise about X. Just before this happens, the tree trunk is about to leave the stone at Y and therefore F<sub>Y</sub> = 0</p>","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 2  |  Chapter 205  |  Source PDF p. 17, 18","source":{"name":"205 Examples.docx","sha256":"215c6a15e4c92f2dd297cc2ee7e95a57a309760a3a81d9ef8f88b21a4fef01d1","block":44},"id":"ex-205-4","chapter":"205"},"ex-205-5":{"number":"5","title":"Forces acting on a ladder","question":"<p>A ladder <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>Y</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> of length 3 m leans against a smooth wall and remains at rest. It just reaches a window 2 m above the ground. The ladder of mass 5 kg is a uniform symmetrical object.</p><p>Find the normal force and friction acting on the ladder by the ground.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/358c9cf8b3eeb32b0d01a709.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:229.7px\"/></p>","answer":"<p>The free-body diagram for the ladder is shown on the right.</p><p>Consider the vertical direction. Take upwards as positive.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>F</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>e</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = 0</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>g</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> + (−<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>g</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>) = 0</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>g</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> − 5 × 9.81 = 0</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/ed1e0367da90edacca7fff0d.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:202.2px\"/></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>g</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = 49.05 N</p><p>≈ 49.1 N</p><p>Take moment about <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>.</p><p>Total clockwise moments = total anticlockwise moment</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>f</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>+</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>g</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mrow><mtext>−</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>g</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><msqrt><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mrow><mtext>−</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math></p><p>2<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>f</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> + 5 × 9.81 × <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 49.05 × <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>f</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 27.4 N</p><p>The normal force is 49.1 N (upwards) and the</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/675c803c5f229996b2df26dc.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:144.7px\"/></p><p>friction is 27.4 N (towards left).</p>","tips":"<p><b>Tip</b></p><p>As end Y of the ladder tends to slide to the right, the friction points to the left.</p>","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 2  |  Chapter 205  |  Source PDF p. 18","source":{"name":"205 Examples.docx","sha256":"215c6a15e4c92f2dd297cc2ee7e95a57a309760a3a81d9ef8f88b21a4fef01d1","block":80},"id":"ex-205-5","chapter":"205"}}